# Line endings — DEF-018-002, DEF-027-004.
#
# Three governance checks compared file content and were, in effect, comparing
# operating systems:
#
#   G-02b     steering currency — `git show HEAD:<file>` (LF) vs the working copy
#   T549a     the frozen egress control — a recorded sha256 vs a hash of the file
#   G-27-11   the EPIC-027 register digests
#
# Git stores blobs with LF. Without this file and with `core.autocrlf=true`, a
# Windows checkout writes CRLF, so both sides differed for a reason no person
# introduced. The checks passed in CI, where a Linux checkout is LF, and failed
# on the machine they were authored on the next time anyone switched branches.
#
# Each was fixed by normalising line endings at the comparison. This file removes
# the class instead of treating it a fourth time: every text file is stored AND
# checked out as LF, on every platform, so a hash means "this content" rather
# than "this content on this operating system".
#
# The per-check normalisations stay. They are cheap, and a check that only works
# because a config file is present is a check with a hidden dependency.

# Detect text automatically; store and check out with LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- binary
#
# Declared explicitly rather than left to detection. `text=auto` would classify
# these correctly, but the SRS `.docx` files are the source material this entire
# programme traces to (Constitution II) — an unreviewable corruption there is not
# a risk worth carrying on a heuristic.

*.docx binary
*.doc  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pdf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

